        "model": "bai/qwen3.8-flash",
        "v": 5,
        "title": "Native tool calls replace text parsing in agent runtime",
        "summary": "Agent runtime switches from custom text-based tool call parsing to native tool calls, updating `parse-tool-calls-from-text` logic and tool parameter definitions in `common/src/tools/params`. The `use-send-message` hook now requires `parentAgentId` to correctly attribute subagent tool calls, and `add-message` output schema changes from `emptyToolResultSchema` to `textToolResultSchema`. This affects CLI, shared core, and agent definitions like `editor-best-of-n` which now use `set_output` and improved spawn result extraction.",
        "significance": "notable",
        "at": "2026-09-15T04:39:42.857Z"
      },
      "eli5": {
        "text": "When you interact with multi-step AI agents (like the best-of-n editor), those agents used to read each other's responses as plain text and try to guess what tools were being called. Now they talk directly using native tool calls, so there's no guessing game, the system knows exactly which helper is being invoked and with what input. You'll notice subagent tool calls are now correctly attributed to the agent that spawned them, and results from spawned helpers come back as clean structured data instead of messy text that needs to be parsed. This makes agent chains like editor-best-of-n more reliable and easier to debug.",
        "model": "vyce/deepseek-v4.1",
